# The OpenCL template in Resolve Studio 21.2.0.5, 2026-11-27 (backlog row 45).
#
# Two levels, kept apart because they answer different questions:
#
#   device level  the plug-in's OpenCL kernels on this machine's RTX 3060,
#                 through the fake host: 118 contract checks x 3 host profiles
#                 and 38 suite checks, all passing
#                 (evidence/2026-11-26-windows/opencl-suite.log). Not re-run here.
#   host level    this file: a real host launched the bundle, applied the effect
#                 and rendered one 1920x1080 frame. The question was what that
#                 host passed into render().

## 1. how the host's answer was made observable

`OFX::Log` writes nothing in a release build (the `fopen` sits behind `#ifdef
DEBUG` in `Support/Library/ofxsLog.cpp`), and a CPU render and a refused GPU
render produce the same pixels. So the measurement is a probe inside a scratch
copy of the template (`.validation/opencl-probe/`, built against `.ofx-ref`,
never committed). The probe appends one line per call to the file named in
`$OFXCL_PROBE`:

    describe opencl_property=accepted|rejected [reason=...]
    describe cpu_property=... / describe nosa_property=...
    describeInContext begin|end
    render t=... opencl=0|1 queue=null|non-null cuda=0|1 metal=0|1 srcRowBytes=... window=WxH
    path=opencl|path=cpu

The line is written before the plug-in branches, so a render that is refused
afterwards still leaves its arguments behind.

